# Code Reuse

## Objective

Search for and reuse existing utilities, components, and patterns before writing new ones that duplicate them.

## Core Principles

- Search the codebase for an existing helper/util before writing a new one.
- Extend an existing component/function when reasonable instead of forking it.
- Shared logic belongs in a shared location, not copy-pasted per call site.
- Don't reinvent something a project dependency already provides.

## Requirements

- Grep for similar function names/behavior before implementing something that 'feels' new.
- If similar logic already exists in two places, consider consolidating rather than adding a third copy.
- Prefer composing existing small functions over duplicating their internals.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Writing a second date-formatting helper because the first one wasn't found.
- Copy-pasting a validation block instead of extracting/reusing it.
- Reimplementing a utility already provided by an existing dependency.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ An existing-code search was actually done before adding new utility code.
✓ No near-duplicate function/component was introduced.
✓ Opportunities to consolidate duplicated logic were at least noted.

## Golden Rule

The fastest code to maintain is the code you didn't have to write twice.
